    {
      "dom_id": "question-6423",
      "name": null,
      "problem": "A triangle has sides $m$, $n$, and $o.$ The sides $m$, $n$, and $o$ are of lengths  $2$, $5$, and $6$, respectively. What is the measure of the angle between the sides $n$ and $o?$ Round your answer to two decimal places.",
      "worked_solution": "The following triangle illustrates the information given.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14252321/assets/26f2041aec84bb0086e2f08c2ba3a19331895951782b7fda153858643a8a5dd4.png)\n\nWe use the Law of Cosines to solve for angle $M:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡M & =\\frac{{o}^{2}+{n}^{2}-{m}^{2}}{2no}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nHere, we have\n\n$$\nm=2,\\,n=5,\\,o=6.\n$$\n\nThis gives \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡M & =\\frac{{6}^{2}+{5}^{2}-{2}^{2}}{2(5)(6)} \\\\  & =\\frac{36+25-4}{60} \\\\  & =\\frac{57}{60}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠M & =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{57}{60}) \\\\  & ={18.19}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places.",
